    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. so it's all about those Form V crystals. does the temperature itself affect how quickly they form, or is it more about the seeding process? any specific temperature stages you'd recommend for a dark chocolate with high cocoa mass?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The temperature dictates the *rate* at which the Form V crystals will form, but the seeding process guides the *selection* of those crystals. You must heat to melt all existing crystals, then carefully cool to let the most stable Form V crystals begin to nucleate, often by adding some tempered chocolate (seeding). For a high cocoa mass dark chocolate, aim to melt around 115-120°F, cool to about 80°F, and then rapidly work it up to your working temperature, usually around 88-91°F.",
      "source": "target",
